<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s09e04" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 4: Intercepts</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 4 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the x- and y-intercepts from a graph
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the x- and y-intercepts from an equation
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 4 Key Terminology</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            x-intercept
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            y-intercept
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <definition xml:id="def-x-intercept">
    <statement>
      <p>
        An <term>x-intercept</term> of a graph is a point where the graph crosses the x-axis
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <example>
  <p>
    Identify all the x-intercepts of the graph pictured below.
  </p>
    <image width="60%">
  <shortdescription>A plot of y = x cubed minus x  </shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-94ex1a" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
                  <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x)=(x^3-x)/4 </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-3,-3,3,3)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-2,1,2)" vticks="(-2,1,2)" hlabels="(-2,1,2)" vlabels="(-2,1,2)"/>
          <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          Solution: The x-axis is the horizontal axis. The graph crosses the x-axis at three points.
        </p>
        <image width="60%">
  <shortdescription>A plot of y = x cubed minus x with the x-intercepts (-1,0), (0,0) and (1,0)</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-94ex1b" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
                  <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x)=(x^3-x)/4 </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-3,-3,3,3)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-2,1,2)" vticks="(-2,1,2)" hlabels="(-2,1,2)" vlabels="(-2,1,2)"/>
        <point p="(-1,0)" stroke = "blue" fill = "blue"/>
          <point p="(0,0)" stroke = "blue" fill = "blue"/>
            <point p="(1,0)" stroke = "blue" fill = "blue"/>
          <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
  </example>
  <p>
    The coordinates of these points are (-1,0), (0,0), and (1,0). So these three points are the x-intercepts of the graph.
  </p>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Notice Something Neat!</title>
    <p>
      Notice that x-intercepts always lie on the x-axis, so they always have a y-coordinate <m>y=0</m>. 
      We can use this fact to find x-intercepts directly from an equation. 
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <example>
    <p>
      Find the x-intercept of the graph of the equation:
      <md>
        y=2x-6
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      An x-intercept is a point which has y-coordinate y=0. So let’s plug y=0 into the equation:
      <md>
        0=2x-6
      </md>
      Now, we can solve this equation for <m>x</m> to get the point’s x-coordinate:
      <md>
        0+6 = 2x-6+6
      </md>
      <md>
      6 = 2x
      </md>
      <md>
        \frac{6}{2} = \frac{2x}{2}
      </md>
      <md>
        3=x
      </md>
      So, the equation’s graph has an x-intercept at <m>(x,y)=(3,0)</m>.
    </p>
  </example>
  <definition xml:id="def-y-intercept">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <term>y-intercept</term> of a graph is point where the graph crosses the y-axis.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <example>
    <p>
      Find the y-intercept of the graph pictured below:
    </p>
    <image width="60%">
  <shortdescription>A plot of y = 2 minus x squared</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-94ex2a" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
                  <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x)=2-x^2 </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-3,-3,3,3)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-2,1,2)" vticks="(-2,1,2)" hlabels="(-2,1,2)" vlabels="(-2,1,2)"/>
                <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          The y-axis is the vertical axis. The graph crosses the vertical axis at one point.
        </p>
        <image width="60%">
  <shortdescription>A plot of y = x cubed minus x with the x-intercepts (-1,0), (0,0) and (1,0)</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-94ex2b" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
                  <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x)=(x^3-x)/4 </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-3,-3,3,3)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-2,1,2)" vticks="(-2,1,2)" hlabels="(-2,1,2)" vlabels="(-2,1,2)"/>
        <point p="(0,2)" stroke = "blue" fill = "blue"/>
          <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          The coordinates of this point are (0,2). So this is the (only) y-intercept of this graph.
        </p>
  </example>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Notice Something Neat!</title>
    <p>
      Notice that y-intercepts lie on the y-axis, so they always have an x-coordinate of <m>x=0</m>. This allows us to find intercepts directly from an equation.
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <example>
    <p>
      Find the y-intercept of the graph of the equation
      <md>
        y=3x^2-1
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      A y-intercept has an x-coordinate of <m>x=0</m>, so plug in <m>x=0</m>:
      <md>
        y = 3(0)^2 - 1
      </md>
      Since <m>y</m> is already isolated on one side of this equation, we don’t have to solve for <m>y</m>. We just have to finish the calculations, using PEMDAS:
      <md>
        y=3(0)-1
      </md>
      <md>
      y=0-1
      </md>
      <md>
        y = -1
      </md>
      So, the y-intercept of the graph of this equation is located at <m>(x,y)=(0-1)</m>
    </p>
  </example>

  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s09e04">
    <title>Season 9, Episode 4 Practice Problems</title>
    <p>
      Find all intercepts for the following graphs.
    </p>
    <sidebyside>
      <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <image width="40%">
  <shortdescription>A plot of y = (x-2)^2(x+3)/3 </shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-94ws1" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
                  <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x)=(x-2)^2*(x+3)/3 </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
          <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
      </exercise>
       <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <image width="40%">
  <shortdescription>A plot of y = 0.5*(x^3+4*x^2+x-6) </shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-94ws2" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
                  <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x)=0.5*(x^3+4*x^2+x-6) </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
          <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
      </exercise>
    </sidebyside>

    <sidebyside>
      <exercise workspace="2.5in">
        <image width="40%">
  <shortdescription>A plot of y = (x+3)(x-2)^2(x+1)^3/10  </shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-94ws3" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
                  <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x)=0.1*(x+3)*(x-2)^2*(x+1)^3 </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
          <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
      </exercise>
       <exercise workspace="2.5in">
        <image width="40%">
  <shortdescription>A plot of y = x cubed </shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-94ws4" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
                  <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x)=1/30*(x+3)*(x-2)^2*(x-5) </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
          <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
      </exercise>
    </sidebyside>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Find the x-intercept of the following equations
        </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y=3x+9</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y=-2x-8</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y=\frac{1}{2}x+3</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y=-\frac{2}{3}x+4</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.0in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y=10x-\frac{1}{5}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Find the y-intercept of the following equations
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="0.7in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y=2x^2-3x+5 </m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.7in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y=x^3 - 12x </m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.7in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y=(2x-3)^2 </m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.7in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y=\sqrt{3x+1} </m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.7in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y=(x-3)^2-5 </m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.7in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y=\frac{1}{2x-1} </m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </worksheet>
</section>  